(defalias 'char-fold-update-table #[0 "\306\300!\203\n␀\206␀	\306\302!\203␀\n\206␀\306\304!\203 ␀\f\206!␀\306\307!\203,␀\206.␀	F\n\232?\205?␀\310 \211\211\n\207" [char-fold-override char-fold--default-override char-fold-include char-fold--default-include char-fold-exclude char-fold--default-exclude boundp char-fold-symmetric char-fold--make-table char-fold--default-symmetric char-fold--previous char-fold-table] 5 (#$ . 316901)])#@473 Non-nil means to override the default definitions of equivalent characters.
When nil (the default), the table of character equivalences used
for character-folding is populated with the default set of equivalent
characters; customize `char-fold-exclude' to remove unneeded equivalences,
and `char-fold-include' to add your own.
When this variable is non-nil, the table of equivalences starts empty,
and you can add your own equivalences by customizing `char-fold-include'.#@2 #@16 

(fn SYM VAL)#@114 Additional character foldings to include.
Each entry is a list of a character and the strings that fold into it.#@135 Character foldings to remove from default decompositions.
Each entry is a list of a character and the strings to remove from folding.#@346 Non-nil means char-fold searching treats equivalent chars the same.
That is, use of any of a set of char-fold equivalent chars in a search
string finds any of them in the text being searched.

If nil then only the "base" or "canonical" char of the set matches
any of them.  The others match only themselves, even when char-folding
is turned on.#@48 Return a string that matches N spaces.

