#@85 Coerce OBJECT to type TYPE.
TYPE is a Common Lisp type specifier.

(fn OBJECT TYPE)#@235 Return t if two Lisp objects have similar structures and contents.
This is like `equal', except that it accepts numerically equal
numbers of different types (float vs. integer), and also compares
strings case-insensitively.

(fn X Y)#@38 

(fn CL-FUNC CL-SEQS &optional ACC)#@137 Map a FUNCTION across one or more SEQUENCEs, returning a sequence.
TYPE is the sequence type to return.

(fn TYPE FUNCTION SEQUENCE...)#@169 Map FUNCTION to each sublist of LIST or LISTs.
Like `cl-mapcar', except applies to lists and their cdr's rather than to
the elements themselves.

(fn FUNCTION LIST...)#@103 Like `cl-mapcar', but does not accumulate values returned by the function.

(fn FUNCTION SEQUENCE...)#@100 Like `cl-maplist', but does not accumulate values returned by the function.

(fn FUNCTION LIST...)#@104 Like `cl-mapcar', but nconc's together the values returned by the function.

(fn FUNCTION SEQUENCE...)#@101 Like `cl-maplist', but nconc's together the values returned by the function.

(fn FUNCTION LIST...)#@239 Say whether PREDICATE is true for any element in the SEQ sequences.
More specifically, the return value of this function will be the
same as the first return value of PREDICATE where PREDICATE has a
non-nil value.


(fn PREDICATE SEQ...)#@90 Return true if PREDICATE is true of every element of SEQ or SEQs.

(fn PREDICATE SEQ...)#@91 Return true if PREDICATE is false of every element of SEQ or SEQs.

