(fn X)#@149 Return a list of the floor of X and the fractional part of X.
With two arguments, return floor and remainder of their quotient.

(fn X &optional Y)#@153 Return a list of the ceiling of X and the fractional part of X.
With two arguments, return ceiling and remainder of their quotient.

(fn X &optional Y)#@161 Return a list of the integer part of X and the fractional part of X.
With two arguments, return truncation and remainder of their quotient.

(fn X &optional Y)#@159 Return a list of X rounded to the nearest integer and the remainder.
With two arguments, return rounding and remainder of their quotient.

(fn X &optional Y)#@69 The remainder of X divided by Y, with the same sign as Y.

(fn X Y)#@69 The remainder of X divided by Y, with the same sign as X.

(fn X Y)#@63 Return 1 if X is positive, -1 if negative, 0 if zero.

(fn X)#@399 Parse integer from the substring of STRING from START to END.
STRING may be surrounded by whitespace chars (chars with syntax ` ').
Other non-digit chars are considered junk.
RADIX is an integer between 2 and 36, the default is 10.  Signal
an error if the substring between START and END cannot be parsed
as an integer unless JUNK-ALLOWED is non-nil.

(fn STRING &key START END RADIX JUNK-ALLOWED)#@157 Return a pseudo-random nonnegative number less than LIM, an integer or float.
Optional second arg STATE is a random-state object.

(fn LIM &optional STATE)#@167 Return a copy of random-state STATE, or of the internal state if omitted.
If STATE is t, return a new state object seeded from the time of day.

(fn &optional STATE)#@315 Initialize the Common Lisp floating-point parameters.
This sets the values of: `cl-most-positive-float', `cl-most-negative-float',
`cl-least-positive-float', `cl-least-negative-float', `cl-float-epsilon',
`cl-float-negative-epsilon', `cl-least-positive-normalized-float', and
`cl-least-negative-normalized-float'.#@309 Return the subsequence of SEQ from START to END.
If END is omitted, it defaults to the length of the sequence.
If START or END is negative, it counts from the end.
Signal an error if START or END are outside of the sequence (i.e
too large if positive or too small if negative).

(fn SEQ START &optional END)#@91 Concatenate, into a sequence of type TYPE, the argument SEQUENCEs.

(fn TYPE SEQUENCE...)#@49 Equivalent to (append (reverse X) Y).

(fn X Y)#@49 Equivalent to (nconc (nreverse X) Y).

