(fn SYMBOL)
(defalias 'let-alist--remove-dot #[257 "\300!\301\302\"\203␀\303\304\305\306\211$!\207\207" [symbol-name string-match "\\`\\." intern replace-match "" nil] 8 (#$ . 1201)])#@1373 Let-bind dotted symbols to their cdrs in ALIST and execute BODY.
Dotted symbol is any symbol starting with a `.'.  Only those present
in BODY are let-bound and this search is done at compile time.

For instance, the following code

  (let-alist alist
    (if (and .title .body)
        .body
      .site
      .site.contents))

essentially expands to

  (let ((.title (cdr (assq \='title alist)))
        (.body  (cdr (assq \='body alist)))
        (.site  (cdr (assq \='site alist)))
        (.site.contents (cdr (assq \='contents (cdr (assq \='site alist))))))
    (if (and .title .body)
        .body
      .site
      .site.contents))

If you nest `let-alist' invocations, the inner one can't access
the variables of the outer one.  You can, however, access alists
inside the original alist by using dots inside the symbol, as
displayed in the example above.

To refer to a non-`let-alist' variable starting with a dot in BODY, use
two dots instead of one.  For example, in the following form `..foo'
refers to the variable `.foo' bound outside of the `let-alist':

    (let ((.foo 42)) (let-alist \='((foo . nil)) ..foo))

Note that there is no way to differentiate the case where a key
is missing from when it is present, but its value is nil.  Thus,
the following form evaluates to nil:

    (let-alist \='((some-key . nil))
      .some-key)

(fn ALIST &rest BODY)#@10 

(fn X)
