#@639 Toggle use of Ibuffer's auto-update facility (Ibuffer Auto mode).

This is a minor mode.  If called interactively, toggle the `Ibuffer-Auto
mode' mode.  If the prefix argument is positive, enable the mode, and if
it is zero or negative, disable the mode.

If called from Lisp, toggle the mode if ARG is `toggle'.  Enable the
mode if ARG is nil, omitted, or is a positive number.  Disable the mode
if ARG is a negative number.

To check whether the minor mode is enabled in the current buffer,
evaluate the variable `ibuffer-auto-mode'.

The mode's hook is called both when the mode is enabled and when it is
disabled.

(fn &optional ARG)#@77 Enable or disable filtering by the major mode chosen via mouse.

(fn EVENT)#@78 Enable or disable filtering by the major mode at point.

(fn EVENT-OR-POINT)#@82 Toggle the display status of the filter group chosen with the mouse.

(fn EVENT)#@61 Toggle the display status of the filter group on this line.#@70 Move point forwards by COUNT filtering groups.

(fn &optional COUNT)#@71 Move point backwards by COUNT filtering groups.

(fn &optional COUNT)#@48 Perform a `isearch-forward' in marked buffers.#@55 Perform a `isearch-forward-regexp' in marked buffers.#@197 Return non-nil if BUF passes all FILTERS.

BUF is a Lisp buffer object, and FILTERS is a list of filter
specifications with the same structure as
`ibuffer-filtering-qualifiers'.

(fn BUF FILTERS)#@61 Make the current filters into a filtering group.

(fn NAME)#@50 Set the current filter groups to filter by mode.#@32 Remove the first filter group.#@67 Decompose the filter group GROUP into active filters.

(fn GROUP)#@27 Remove all filter groups.#@63 Move point to the filter group whose name is NAME.

(fn NAME)#@107 Kill the filter group named NAME.
The group will be added to `ibuffer-filter-group-kill-ring'.

(fn NAME)#@105 Kill the filter group at point.
See also `ibuffer-kill-filter-group'.

(fn &optional ARG INTERACTIVE-P)#@58 Yank the last killed filter group before group at point.#@71 Yank the last killed filter group before group named NAME.

(fn NAME)#@176 Save all active filter groups GROUPS as NAME.
They are added to `ibuffer-saved-filter-groups'.  Interactively,
prompt for NAME, and use the current filters.

(fn NAME GROUPS)#@103 Delete saved filter groups with NAME.
They are removed from `ibuffer-saved-filter-groups'.

(fn NAME)#@126 Set this buffer's filter groups to saved version with NAME.
The value from `ibuffer-saved-filter-groups' is used.

(fn NAME)#@221 Disable all filters currently in effect in this buffer.
With optional arg DELETE-FILTER-GROUPS non-nil, delete all filter
group definitions by setting `ibuffer-filter-groups' to nil.

(fn &optional DELETE-FILTER-GROUPS)#@39 Remove the top filter in this buffer.#@278 Separate this buffer's top compound filter (AND, OR, NOT, or SAVED).

This means that the topmost filter on the filtering stack, which must
be a complex filter like (OR [name: foo] [mode: bar-mode]), will be
turned into separate filters, like [name: foo] and [mode: bar-mode].#@59 Exchange the top two filters on the stack in this buffer.#@59 Negate the sense of the top filter in the current buffer.#@180 Replace the top two filters in this buffer with their logical OR.
If optional argument DECOMPOSE is non-nil, instead break the top OR
filter into parts.

(fn &optional DECOMPOSE)#@182 Replace the top two filters in this buffer with their logical AND.
If optional argument DECOMPOSE is non-nil, instead break the top AND
filter into parts.

(fn &optional DECOMPOSE)#@152 Save FILTERS in this buffer with name NAME in `ibuffer-saved-filters'.
Interactively, prompt for NAME, and use the current filters.

(fn NAME FILTERS)#@73 Delete saved filters with NAME from `ibuffer-saved-filters'.

(fn NAME)#@85 Add saved filters from `ibuffer-saved-filters' to this buffer's filters.

(fn NAME)#@89 Set this buffer's filters to filters with NAME from `ibuffer-saved-filters'.

