(defvar minibuffer-depth-indicate-mode nil (#$ . 807236))#@885 Toggle Minibuffer Depth Indication mode.

Minibuffer Depth Indication mode is a global minor mode.  When
enabled, any recursive use of the minibuffer will show the
recursion depth in the minibuffer prompt.  This is only useful if
`enable-recursive-minibuffers' is non-nil.

This is a global minor mode.  If called interactively, toggle the
`Minibuffer-Depth-Indicate mode' mode.  If the prefix argument is
positive, enable the mode, and if it is zero or negative, disable the
mode.

If called from Lisp, toggle the mode if ARG is `toggle'.  Enable the
mode if ARG is nil, omitted, or is a positive number.  Disable the mode
if ARG is a negative number.

To check whether the minor mode is enabled in the current buffer,
evaluate `(default-value \='minibuffer-depth-indicate-mode)'.

The mode's hook is called both when the mode is enabled and when it is
disabled.

(fn &optional ARG)#@205 Generate a report of how Emacs is using memory.

This report is approximate, and will commonly over-count memory
usage by variables, because shared data structures will usually
by counted more than once.#@131 Major mode for editing mail and news to be sent.
Like `text-mode', but with these additional commands:

\{message-mode-map}

(fn)#@359 Start editing a mail message to be sent.
OTHER-HEADERS is an alist of header/value pairs.  CONTINUE says whether
to continue editing a message already being composed.  SWITCH-FUNCTION
is a function used to switch to and display the mail buffer.

(fn &optional TO SUBJECT OTHER-HEADERS CONTINUE SWITCH-FUNCTION YANK-ACTION SEND-ACTIONS RETURN-ACTION &rest _)#@77 Start editing a news article to be sent.

(fn &optional NEWSGROUPS SUBJECT)#@109 Start editing a reply to the article in the current buffer.

(fn &optional TO-ADDRESS WIDE SWITCH-FUNCTION)#@86 Make a "wide" reply to the message in the current buffer.

(fn &optional TO-ADDRESS)#@134 Follow up to the message in the current buffer.
If TO-NEWSGROUPS, use that as the new Newsgroups line.

(fn &optional TO-NEWSGROUPS)#@102 Cancel an article you posted.
If ARG, allow editing of the cancellation message.

(fn &optional ARG)#@168 Start composing a message to supersede the current message.
This is done simply by taking the old article and adding a Supersedes
header line with the old Message-ID.#@65 Reread contents of current buffer from its last auto-save file.#@167 Forward the current message via mail.
Optional NEWS will use news to forward instead of mail.
Optional DIGEST will use digest to forward.

(fn &optional NEWS DIGEST)#@40 

(fn FORWARD-BUFFER &optional DIGEST)#@23 

(fn FORWARD-BUFFER)#@35 Let RMAIL use message to forward.#@54 Resend the current article to ADDRESS.

(fn ADDRESS)#@172 Re-mail the current message.
This only makes sense if the current message is a bounce message that
contains some mail you have written which has been bounced back to
you.#@100 Like `message-mail' command, but display mail buffer in another window.

(fn &optional TO SUBJECT)#@99 Like `message-mail' command, but display mail buffer in another frame.

(fn &optional TO SUBJECT)#@189 Bold all nonblank characters in the region.
Works by overstriking characters.
Called from program, takes two arguments START and END
which specify the range to operate on.

(fn START END)#@170 Remove all boldness (overstruck characters) in the region.
Called from program, takes two arguments START and END
which specify the range to operate on.

(fn START END)#@734 Command to parse command line mailto: links.
This is meant to be used for MIME handlers: Setting the handler
for "x-scheme-handler/mailto;" to "emacs -f message-mailto %u"
will then start up Emacs ready to compose mail.  For emacsclient use
  emacsclient -e \='(message-mailto "%u")'

To facilitate the use of this function within window systems that
provide message subject, body and attachments independent of URL
itself, the arguments SUBJECT, BODY and FILE-ATTACHMENTS may also
provide alternative message subject and body text, which is
inserted in lieu of nothing if URL does not incorporate such
information itself, and a list of files to insert as attachments
to the E-mail.

(fn &optional URL SUBJECT BODY FILE-ATTACHMENTS)#@48 Major mode for editing Metafont sources.

(fn)#@48 Major mode for editing MetaPost sources.

(fn)#@94 Compose a message with the MH mail system.
See `mh-send' for more details on composing mail.#@110 Compose a message with the MH mail system in other window.
See `mh-send' for more details on composing mail.#@509 Compose a message with the MH mail system.

This function does not prompt the user for any header fields, and
thus is suitable for use by programs that want to create a mail
buffer. Users should use \[mh-smail] to compose mail.

Optional arguments for setting certain fields include TO,
SUBJECT, and OTHER-HEADERS. Additional arguments are IGNORED.

This function remains for Emacs 21 compatibility. New
applications should use `mh-user-agent-compose'.

(fn &optional TO SUBJECT OTHER-HEADERS &rest IGNORED)#@605 Set up mail composition draft with the MH mail system.
This is the `mail-user-agent' entry point to MH-E. This function
conforms to the contract specified by `define-mail-user-agent'
which means that this function should accept the same arguments
as `compose-mail'.

The optional arguments TO and SUBJECT specify recipients and the
initial Subject field, respectively.

OTHER-HEADERS is an alist specifying additional header fields.
Elements look like (HEADER . VALUE) where both HEADER and VALUE
are strings.

Any additional arguments are IGNORED.

(fn &optional TO SUBJECT OTHER-HEADERS &rest IGNORED)#@1033 Save draft and send message.

When you are all through editing a message, you send it with this
command. You can give a prefix argument ARG to monitor the first stage
of the delivery; this output can be found in a buffer called "*MH-E
Mail Delivery*".

The hook `mh-before-send-letter-hook' is run at the beginning of
this command. For example, if you want to check your spelling in
your message before sending, add the function `ispell-message'.

Unless `mh-insert-auto-fields' had previously been called
manually, the function `mh-insert-auto-fields' is called to
insert fields based upon the recipients. If fields are added, you
are given a chance to see and to confirm these fields before the
message is actually sent. You can do away with this confirmation
by turning off the option `mh-auto-fields-prompt-flag'.

In case the MH "send" program is installed under a different name,
use `mh-send-prog' to tell MH-E the name.

The hook `mh-annotate-msg-hook' is run after annotating the
message and scan line.

(fn &optional ARG)#@252 Quit editing and delete draft message.

If for some reason you are not happy with the draft, you can use
this command to kill the draft buffer and delete the draft
message. Use the command \[kill-buffer] if you don't want to
delete the draft message.
(byte-code "\301\302\303\304#\210\305\302\303\306\307$\210\310\303\311\"\210\310\312\313\"\210\305\314\315\316\307$\210\310\315\317\"\210\320\321\322\323\324\325%\210\305\326\327\330\307$\210\305\322\327\331\307$\210\305\332\327\333\307$\210\305\334\327\335\307$\210\305\336\327\337\307$\210\305\340\327\341\307$\210\305\342\327\343\307$\210\305\344\327\345\307$\210\305\346\327\347\307$\210\305\350\327\351\307$\210\305\352\327\353#\210\305\354\327\355#\210\305\356\327\357\307$\210\305\360\327\361\307$\210\305\362\327\363\307$\210\305\364\327\365\307$\210\305\366\327\367\307$\210\305\370\327\371\307$\210\305\372\327\373\307$\210\305\374\327\375\307$\210\305\376\327\377\307$\210\305\201@␀\327\201A␀\307$\210\310\327\201B␀\"\210\201C␀\201D␀!B\305\201E␀\201F␀\201G␀\307$\210\305\201H␀\201F␀\201I␀\307$\210\310\201F␀\201J␀\"\210\310\201K␀\201L␀\"\210\310\201M␀\201N␀\"\210\310\201O␀\201P␀\"\210\305\201Q␀\201R␀\201S␀\307$\210\305\201T␀\201R␀\201U␀\307$\210\305\201V␀\201R␀\201W␀#\210\320\201X␀\201Y␀\201Z␀\201[␀\201\\␀%\210\305\201Y␀\201R␀\201]␀#\210\305\201Z␀\201R␀\201^␀\307$\210\305\201[␀\201R␀\201_␀\307$\210\310\201R␀\201`␀\"\210\201C␀\201a␀!B\201b␀\201c␀\201d␀\307#\207" [package--builtin-versions custom-autoload minibuffer-depth-indicate-mode "mb-depth" nil autoload (#$ . 810032) t register-definition-prefixes ("minibuffer-depth-") "md4" ("md4") memory-report "memory-report" (#$ . 810922) ("memory-report-") define-mail-user-agent message-user-agent message-mail message-send-and-exit message-kill-buffer message-send-hook message-mode "message" (#$ . 811132) (#$ . 811268) message-news (#$ . 811631) message-reply (#$ . 811713) message-wide-reply (#$ . 811826) message-followup (#$ . 811917) message-cancel-news (#$ . 812056) message-supersede (#$ . 812163) message-recover (#$ . 812335) message-forward (#$ . 812405) message-forward-make-body (#$ . 812576) message-forward-rmail-make-body (#$ . 812620) message-insinuate-rmail (#$ . 812647) message-resend (#$ . 812686) message-bounce (#$ . 812745) message-mail-other-window (#$ . 812922) message-mail-other-frame (#$ . 813026) message-news-other-window (#$ . 811631) message-news-other-frame (#$ . 811631) message-bold-region (#$ . 813130) message-unbold-region (#$ . 813324) message-mailto (#$ . 813499) ("message-") purecopy (meta-mode 1 0) metafont-mode "meta-mode" (#$ . 814237) metapost-mode (#$ . 814289) ("meta") "mh-acros" ("mh-" "with-mh-folder-updating") "mh-alias" ("mh-") "mh-buffers" ("mh-") mh-smail "mh-comp" (#$ . 814341) mh-smail-other-window (#$ . 814440) mh-smail-batch (#$ . 814555) mh-e-user-agent mh-user-agent-compose mh-send-letter mh-fully-kill-draft mh-before-send-letter-hook (#$ . 815069) (#$ . 815680) (#$ . 816718) ("mh-") (mh-e 8 6 -4) put mh-progs risky-local-variable] 6)#@73 Display version information about MH-E and the MH mail handling system.#@173 Incorporate new mail with MH.
Scan an MH folder if ARG is non-nil.

This function is an entry point to MH-E, the Emacs interface to
